## Langfuse connects experiment trade-offs to the individual regressions behind them

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-19-experiments
Published: 2026-10-19
Channel: news

Langfuse has updated its Experiments page to make quality, cost, and latency comparisons easier to investigate together. Teams can chart metrics across runs and read summary statistics for each score relative to a baseline. The change matters because an aggregate improvement can conceal a regression elsewhere: a cheaper candidate is not necessarily a better candidate if the quality measure that matters to the application deteriorates.

The comparison view connects those run-level changes to individual dataset items. Reviewers can filter scores below a threshold within one run or across runs, select the metrics displayed as columns, and adjust row height for longer outputs. Keyboard navigation supports adding a human score, corrected output, or comment without leaving the comparison. Operators can therefore move from spotting a changed metric to inspecting the examples behind it in the same workflow. The announcement describes review capabilities, not an automatic release gate or proof that any particular evaluator is reliable.

Why it matters: Use run-level comparisons to locate trade-offs, then inspect failing examples before accepting a candidate.

